title: Computer Science Fundamentals
subject: Computer Science
level: GCSE
emoji: 💻
description: Hardware, binary, networks, security and algorithms.
---
What does CPU stand for and what does it do? :: Central Processing Unit – fetches, decodes and executes instructions.
What are the three stages of the fetch–decode–execute cycle? :: Fetch an instruction from memory, decode it, then execute it.
What does RAM do? :: Volatile main memory that temporarily stores data and programs currently in use.
What does ROM do? :: Non-volatile memory that stores the boot-up instructions (BIOS).
What is the difference between volatile and non-volatile memory? :: Volatile memory loses its contents when power is off; non-volatile keeps them.
What is the ALU? :: The Arithmetic Logic Unit – performs calculations and logical comparisons.
What is cache memory? :: Very fast memory inside or near the CPU that stores frequently used data.
How many bits are in a byte? :: 8
How many bytes are in a kilobyte, megabyte, gigabyte? :: 1 KB = 1000 B, 1 MB = 1000 KB, 1 GB = 1000 MB (decimal, as in GCSE).
Convert binary 1010 to denary. :: 10
Convert denary 13 to binary. :: 1101
What is hexadecimal used for? :: A compact way to represent binary (e.g. colour codes, memory addresses).
What is ASCII? :: A character set that represents text characters using 7-bit binary codes.
What is a pixel? :: The smallest individual point of an image.
What is lossy compression? :: Reduces file size by permanently removing some data (e.g. JPEG, MP3).
What is a LAN vs a WAN? :: LAN covers a small area (e.g. a school); WAN covers a large geographical area (e.g. the Internet).
What does an IP address identify? :: A device on a network.
What is phishing? :: Fraudulent emails or messages that trick people into revealing sensitive data.
What is a firewall? :: Software or hardware that monitors and blocks unauthorised network traffic.
What is an algorithm? :: A step-by-step set of instructions to solve a problem.
How does a binary search work? :: Repeatedly halves a sorted list by checking the middle item.
How does a bubble sort work? :: Repeatedly compares adjacent items and swaps them if out of order.
What are the three basic programming constructs? :: Sequence, selection and iteration.
What is the difference between a compiler and an interpreter? :: A compiler translates all the code at once; an interpreter translates and runs it line by line.
